---
// src/pages/attitude.astro  →  arizvi.com/attitude
// What the mark is. Reached by clicking the mark in the nav, and from "About the mark" in the footer.
// Laid out like the other main pages (Ali, 11 Nov 2026: "make attitude a nice format like you've done with other pages"):
// the instrument where home has the photo, a section for each part of the mark with its figure beside the text, the
// in-page nav on the right, and the videos as cards, each over its own frame blurred under white.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';

// Each becomes a card in the Flying section. The poster is a frame from the video; it shows before the video loads.
const videos = [
  { title: 'Sunset landing', note: 'Landing at Friday Harbor into a sunset. The video is sped up four times.', url: '/media/flying/sunset-landing.mp4', poster: '/media/flying/sunset-landing.jpg' },
  { title: 'Water landing', note: 'Water landing in Winter Haven, FL. Seaplanes are some of the most fun vehicles in the world!', url: '/media/flying/water-landing.mp4', poster: '/media/flying/water-landing.jpg' },
  { title: 'Seattle by night', note: 'Flying over the Space Needle at night.', url: '/media/flying/seattle.mp4', poster: '/media/flying/seattle.jpg' },
  { title: 'GA in Canada', note: 'How we fly general aviation out of farm strips in Canada.', url: '/media/flying/gaincanada.mp4', poster: '/media/flying/gaincanada.jpg' },
];
const channel = ''; // YouTube channel URL; the "All of the videos" link appears once this is set

const HEX = 'M92 50 L71 86.4 L29 86.4 L8 50 L29 13.6 L71 13.6 Z';
---

<Base title="The curious are rewarded" description="What the mark on this site is, and why." noindex pageNav>
  <section class="intro">
    <div class="intro-grid">
      <div class="text">
        <p class="label">About the mark</p>
        <h1>The curious are rewarded</h1>
        <p>The mark on this site is an <mark>attitude indicator</mark>. I am a pilot, and it is the instrument I look at most. Here is what is going on in it, and why each part is there. The link at the end of each section takes you to the research it points at.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="photo">
        <!-- Same file as public/mark-animated-loop.svg, inlined so it can respect reduced motion. -->
        <svg class="instrument" viewBox="0 0 100 100" role="img" aria-labelledby="mark-title">
          <title id="mark-title">Hexagonal attitude indicator taking off and turning left</title>
          <defs><clipPath id="hx"><path d={HEX} /></clipPath></defs>
          <g clip-path="url(#hx)">
            <g class="ball">
              <animateTransform attributeName="transform" type="rotate" values="0 50 50;0 50 50;0 50 50;22 50 50;22 50 50;0 50 50;0 50 50;0 50 50" keyTimes="0;.12;.28;.44;.6;.72;.86;1" calcMode="spline" keySplines="0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1" dur="9s" repeatCount="indefinite" />
              <animateTransform attributeName="transform" type="translate" additive="sum" values="0 0;0 0;0 18;0 18;0 18;0 18;0 0;0 0" keyTimes="0;.12;.28;.44;.6;.72;.86;1" calcMode="spline" keySplines="0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1;0.42 0 0.58 1" dur="9s" repeatCount="indefinite" />
              <rect x="-80" y="-130" width="260" height="180" fill="#fff" />
              <rect x="-80" y="50" width="260" height="180" fill="#000" />
              <path d="M38 38 H62 M42 26 H58" stroke="#000" stroke-width="2" />
              <path d="M44 44 H56 M44 32 H56" stroke="#000" stroke-width="1.2" />
              <path d="M38 62 H62 M42 74 H58" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2" />
              <path d="M44 56 H56 M44 68 H56" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.2" />
            </g>
          </g>
          <path d="M50 15.6 L46 22.6 H54 Z" fill="#000" />
          <g fill="#000" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2" paint-order="stroke">
            <rect x="27" y="47" width="15" height="6" /><rect x="58" y="47" width="15" height="6" /><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="3.5" />
          </g>
          <path d={HEX} fill="none" stroke="#000" stroke-width="4" stroke-linejoin="miter" />
        </svg>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Section title="It shows an attitude">
    <div class="part">
      <div class="text">
        <p>An attitude indicator draws the horizon the way the aircraft sees it: sky on top, ground below, and a fixed little aircraft symbol in the middle. Nose up -> horizon drops. Bank -> horizon tilts. The mark is frozen at fifteen degrees nose-up, wings level, which is what it shows in the first few seconds after takeoff.</p>
        <p>It is also the oldest answer to the question my thesis asks. Most interfaces assume the room holds still. A cockpit never does, so the instrument moves the world and keeps you fixed. My PhD is basically about that inversion: what happens to technology when the room moves (a van), shrinks (a small apartment), or is not really a room at all (a card table).</p>
        <p class="label crumb"><a href="/projects/phd/">Interaction systems for constrained spaces →</a></p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true">
          <defs><clipPath id="hx-still"><path d={HEX} /></clipPath></defs>
          <g clip-path="url(#hx-still)">
            <g transform="translate(0 18)">
              <rect x="-80" y="-130" width="260" height="180" fill="#fff" />
              <rect x="-80" y="50" width="260" height="180" fill="#000" />
              <path d="M38 38 H62 M42 26 H58" stroke="#000" stroke-width="2" />
              <path d="M44 44 H56 M44 32 H56" stroke="#000" stroke-width="1.2" />
              <path d="M38 62 H62 M42 74 H58" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2" />
              <path d="M44 56 H56 M44 68 H56" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="1.2" />
            </g>
          </g>
          <path d="M50 15.6 L46 22.6 H54 Z" fill="#000" />
          <g fill="#000" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="2" paint-order="stroke">
            <rect x="27" y="47" width="15" height="6" /><rect x="58" y="47" width="15" height="6" /><circle cx="50" cy="50" r="3.5" />
          </g>
          <path d={HEX} fill="none" stroke="#000" stroke-width="4" stroke-linejoin="miter" />
        </svg>
        <figcaption>The mark: fifteen degrees nose-up, wings level.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="It is always day zero">
    <div class="part">
      <div class="text">
        <p>Pilots call the moment the nose comes up for takeoff "rotation." At that point nothing is proven yet, you have the least altitude you will have all flight, and the instrument shows you exactly what you are doing. I like that as a way to work. Every project on this site started at rotation: a question, a field site, and an artifact that did not exist yet. Proteus was a lab prototype before it was a company. Bodhika was a study with nine parents before it was a lab with a school in it. The climb is the part with the most decisions and the least margin, and it is the part I actually enjoy. Day zero, every day.</p>
        <p class="label crumb"><a href="/projects/">The projects, at their current altitude →</a></p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M6 72 H44 L94 28" fill="none" stroke="#000" stroke-width="4" stroke-linejoin="round" />
          <circle cx="44" cy="72" r="6" fill="#000" />
        </svg>
        <figcaption>Rotation, then the climb.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="It is a hexagon because of a chart">
    <div class="part">
      <div class="text">
        <p>Aircraft instruments are round, so why a hexagon? On aeronautical charts, a VOR (the ground beacon that aircraft navigate by) is drawn as a hexagon with a dot in the middle. You fly from one VOR to the next. Each one only tells you where the next one is, and that has been enough to cross continents for seventy years.</p>
        <p>The research has gone the same way: a van -> small homes -> a card table -> a classroom in Hyderabad. I did not plan that route. Each site was just the next beacon in range from the last one.</p>
        <p class="label crumb"><a href="/projects/">Where the research went →</a></p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true">
          <path d="M84 50 L67 79.5 L33 79.5 L16 50 L33 20.5 L67 20.5 Z" fill="none" stroke="#000" stroke-width="4" />
          <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="5" fill="#000" />
        </svg>
        <figcaption>A VOR, as drawn on an aeronautical chart.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Two bars and a dot">
    <div class="part">
      <div class="text">
        <p>On most attitude indicators the aircraft symbol is two short bars and a dot. Those are also the three shapes in the oldest experiment in HCI: two targets and a pointer moving between them. Paul Fitts ran it in 1954. The result (time to reach a target depends on how far away it is and how big it is) still decides how big the buttons on your phone are.</p>
        <p>Fitts kept the targets fixed and measured the pointer. Proteus flips it - the target is programmable. A playing card that can become any other card, on a table where people have been reaching for cards for a few hundred years. The RQ: how much can you change about an object before people stop treating it as the thing they know?</p>
        <p class="label crumb"><a href="/projects/scramble/">The Proteus deck →</a></p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig">
        <svg viewBox="12 40 76 20" aria-hidden="true">
          <rect x="16" y="44" width="24" height="12" fill="#000" />
          <rect x="60" y="44" width="24" height="12" fill="#000" />
          <circle cx="50" cy="50" r="6" fill="#000" />
        </svg>
        <figcaption>The aircraft symbol. Also a Fitts's law task.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="The favicon turns left">
    <div class="part">
      <div class="text">
        <p>In the browser tab the instrument is banked twenty degrees left, with the aircraft symbol removed. At sixteen pixels, two bars and a dot stop looking like an aircraft and start looking like a face (it did, and it was bad). The left turn is not random either - circuits at most airports are flown with left turns, so after takeoff, left is the way you go.</p>
        <p>The face is the same problem the Bodhika study is about, at sixteen pixels. Shrink something or pull it out of context, and people read it as something else. A well-being score on its own reads as a label on the child. The same score inside a whole picture of the child reads as information a parent can act on. Same reason the dashboard got redesigned.</p>
        <p class="label crumb"><a href="/projects/bodhika/">The Bodhika dashboards →</a></p>
      </div>
      <figure class="fig">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true">
          <defs><clipPath id="hx-fav"><path d="M95 50 L72.5 89 L27.5 89 L5 50 L27.5 11 L72.5 11 Z" /></clipPath></defs>
          <rect width="100" height="100" fill="#000" />
          <g clip-path="url(#hx-fav)"><rect x="-60" y="-60" width="220" height="114" fill="#fff" transform="rotate(20 50 50)" /></g>
          <path d="M95 50 L72.5 89 L27.5 89 L5 50 L27.5 11 L72.5 11 Z" fill="none" stroke="#fff" stroke-width="6" />
        </svg>
        <figcaption>Sixteen pixels of left bank.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Flying">
    <div class="grid"><div class="span-8">
      <p>I love flying. Here is some of it on video.</p>
      <ul class="cards flights">
        {videos.map((v) => (
          <li class="card flight">
            <div class="card-bg" style={`background-image:url('${v.poster}')`} aria-hidden="true"></div>
            <video src={v.url} poster={v.poster} controls preload="none" playsinline aria-label={v.title}></video>
            <div class="card-body">
              <h3>{v.title}</h3>
              <p>{v.note}</p>
            </div>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      {channel && <p><a href={channel} rel="noopener">All of the videos</a></p>}
      <p class="label crumb"><a href="/teaching/">What comes next →</a></p>
    </div></div>
  </Section>
</Base>

<style>
  /* The instrument stands where home has the photo. */
  .instrument { display: block; width: 100%; max-width: 15rem; height: auto; margin-left: auto; }
  /* A part of the mark: the text where every page has it, its figure beside it, the right-hand columns free for the
     in-page nav. */
  .part { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(12, minmax(0, 1fr)); column-gap: var(--gutter); align-items: start; }
  .part .text { grid-column: 1 / 7; }
  .part .text p { max-width: none; }
  .fig { grid-column: 7 / 9; margin: 0; }
  .fig svg { display: block; width: 100%; max-width: 9.5rem; height: auto; }
  .fig figcaption { margin-top: 12px; font-size: 13px; line-height: 1.4; max-width: 18ch; }
  .crumb { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  .crumb a { text-decoration: none; }
  .crumb a:hover, .crumb a:focus-visible { text-decoration: underline; text-decoration-thickness: 1px; text-underline-offset: 0.35em; }
  /* The videos as cards: the video at the top, the title and note over its own frame blurred under white. */
  .flights { list-style: none; margin: 1.5rem 0 0; padding: 0; max-width: none; }
  .flights > li { margin: 0; }
  .flight { padding: 0; display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .flight video { display: block; width: 100%; aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; object-fit: contain; background: #000; border-bottom: 2px solid #000; }
  .flight .card-body { padding: 16px 20px 20px; }
  .flight h3 { margin: 0 0 6px; font-size: 1.25rem; }
  .flight p { margin: 0; max-width: none; }
  @media (max-width: 1023px) {
    .part .text { grid-column: 1 / 9; }
    .fig { grid-column: 9 / 12; }
  }
  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .instrument { margin-left: 0; }
    .part .text, .fig { grid-column: 1 / -1; }
    .fig { order: -1; display: flex; align-items: flex-end; gap: 16px; margin-bottom: 1.25rem; }
    .fig svg { width: 5.5rem; flex: none; }
    .fig figcaption { margin: 0; }
  }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .ball { transform: translate(0, 18px); }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Reduced motion: stop the SMIL loop and park in the climb.
  if (window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) {
    document.querySelectorAll('.instrument animateTransform').forEach((a) => a.remove());
  }
</script>
